<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Tafna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-conquest-of-algeria">French conquest of Algeria</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafna">Tafna</a> was signed on 30 May 1837 near the Tafna river between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir-abdelkader">Emir Abdelkader</a> and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-robert-bugeaud">Thomas-Robert Bugeaud</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised Abdelkader's authority over most of western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> while keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and their hinterlands, which freed French troops to attack Constantine. Disputes over the boundary led Abdelkader to resume the war in 1839.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tafna.jpg" alt="Treaty of Tafna" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chatsam · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roubaix_marie_eleonore_godefroid_roubaix_portrait_abdelkader_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the summer of 1836 French forces under Thomas Robert Bugeaud defeated a mixed army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir-abdelkader">Emir Abdelkader</a>'s regular and tribal warriors at the Sikkak river, and letters passed between the two commanders. That year General Clauzel had been defeated in the east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, and the French War Ministry charged Bugeaud with making peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s limited forces could avenge that defeat. Other accounts stress French dissatisfaction with an earlier treaty that had given Abdelkader much control over trade with the French enclaves, a matter of political weight for the merchants of Marseilles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>In its French text Abdelkader recognised French sovereignty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in exchange recognised his rule over about two-thirds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. The Arabic text said only that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> was aware of the rule of French power in Africa; the historian James McDougall argues from Abdelkader's letters that this cannot have been a translation error and that the difference was duplicity on Bugeaud's part. France kept the major ports, through which all of Algeria's trade was to pass, though traders on both sides ignored the rule.</p><p>Bugeaud and Abdelkader also made private agreements. Bugeaud promised modern weapons and the forced removal of the Dawa'ir and Zmala tribes and the exile of their chiefs, for which he received a cash payment that he spent on roadworks in his constituency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The peace allowed the French to concentrate their forces in the east, where they took Constantine on 13 October 1837. Abdelkader consolidated his power over the tribes of the interior, set up a capital at Tagdempt and rebuilt his forces. He claimed under the treaty the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-gates">Iron Gates</a>, a valley commanding the main route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and Constantine; when French troops marched through it in late 1839, he declared the treaty broken and renewed the call for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>. The treaty enhanced Bugeaud's reputation, and the historian Naylor argues that its terms show the French treated Abdelkader's territory as sovereign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Tafna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the east Ahmed Bey of Constantine, whose city fell in 1837 after a failed first assault in 1836, and in the west the Emirate of Abdelkader, which France recognised in the Treaty of Desmichels in 1834 and the <b>Treaty of Tafna</b> in 1837 before war resumed in 1839.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He fought the French and won recognition in the Treaty of Desmichels of 1834 and the <b>Treaty of Tafna</b> of 1837.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-abdelkader">Emirate of Abdelkader</a></li></ul></section>
